### Which @angular/* package(s) are the source of the bug?

core

### Is this a regression?

Not established. A related streaming-resource cancellation report (#64625) was closed by its author without an associated fix.

### Description

When a resource has an outstanding loader and its params become unavailable, the resource immediately changes status but does not abort the obsolete loader or release that loader's pending task. If the obsolete promise never settles, `ApplicationRef.whenStable()` remains pending indefinitely even though the resource is now idle or in a params-error state.

This affects params returning `undefined`, throwing `ResourceParamsStatus.IDLE` or `ResourceParamsStatus.LOADING`, and throwing an error. In the LOADING case, the resource is waiting on upstream params; its obsolete downstream request should no longer own a pending task.

The resource guide says outstanding loads are aborted when params change. This differs from the expected behavior of blocking stability while a valid current request is loading (as discussed in #60742).

### Minimal reproduction

Executable regression tests: [resource_spec.ts](https://github.com/shoutoutuoadi325/angular/blob/a110511b6f383a567dd38d186da3e61fc20f6047/packages/core/test/resource/resource_spec.ts#L467), with unavailable/error status cases in [params_status_spec.ts](https://github.com/shoutoutuoadi325/angular/blob/a110511b6f383a567dd38d186da3e61fc20f6047/packages/core/test/resource/params_status_spec.ts#L24). Run `pnpm bazel test //packages/core/test/resource:resource`; keeping the tests and restoring the pre-fix `resource.ts` reproduces the blocked stability calls.

Use the following as `src/main.ts` in an Angular application, wait for `loader started` in the console, then click **Stop**:

```ts
import {ApplicationRef, Component, inject, resource, signal} from '@angular/core';
import {bootstrapApplication} from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  template: `
    <button (click)="stop()">Stop</button>
    <p>Status: {{data.status()}}; aborted: {{aborted()}}</p>
  `,
})
class App {
  readonly params = signal<number | undefined>(1);
  readonly aborted = signal(false);
  private readonly app = inject(ApplicationRef);
  readonly data = resource({
    params: this.params,
    loader: ({abortSignal}) => {
      console.log('loader started');
      abortSignal.addEventListener('abort', () => this.aborted.set(true));
      // Cancellation must release stability even if a loader never settles.
      return new Promise<string>(() => {});
    },
  });

  stop() {
    this.params.set(undefined);
    void this.app.whenStable().then(() => console.log('stable after stop'));
  }
}

void bootstrapApplication(App);
```

Expected: `Status: idle; aborted: true`, and `stable after stop` is logged.

Actual: `Status: idle; aborted: false`, and the stability promise never resolves.

### Environment

Angular main source commit `fdb4517612cf191da3c3e2df251f9f79f026448b` (`22.3.0-next.3`), macOS arm64. The repository's Node/Bazel resource suite is used to verify the behavior.

### Anything else?

`ResourceImpl.loadEffect()` checks unavailable/stale params before calling `abortInProgressLoad()`. Cancelling before those early returns fixes the omitted cleanup while preserving the existing request identity checks. Regression tests also cover an obsolete promise resolving/rejecting after a new load starts, ensuring the new load retains its pending task and value.

Related: #64625. PR #71557 changes abort reasons/controller retention, but does not fix this early-return path.

### Is this a regression?

- [ ] Yes, this behavior used to work in the previous version

### The previous version in which this bug was not present was

_No response_

### Description

A combobox that filters its listbox's options can select an option that the filter has just removed. If Enter is pressed in the same render as the change that removes the active option (a fast typist, a slow device, or tools that send keys in a burst), the listbox selects the removed option and emits it from `valueChange`. A render later the listbox's own cleanup drops the value again, so `valueChange` fires `['apple']` and then `[]`.

For a consumer that acts on the first emission, the result is a choice the user never saw. In our app a command palette navigated to a page that was not in the list, and a form combobox saved a value that was not in the list (the user typed "ist" and got "Erbil central").

As far as we can tell from the 22.2.3 sources (the same code is in 22.2.4):

- The listbox learns its options through a `MutationObserver` (`_collection.startObserving(this.element)`). Its `items()` therefore lag a DOM change until the observer fires.
- The reset of an active item that is no longer in `items()` runs in a later `afterRenderEffect` (`listbox.mjs`, the effect that calls `listBehavior.unfocus()` and `setDefaultState()`).
- The combobox's relayed Enter is dispatched from its own `afterRenderEffect` (`keyboardEventRelayEffect`). That happens in the render that removed the option, before either of the above has caught up, so the Enter acts on the stale `activeItem`.

Pressing Enter after one more render selects the right option.

Related: #33973 (the same relay, keys merged within a render). It is a separate mechanism, but the two combine: a quick Down + Enter reaches the listbox as a lone Enter on the stale active item.

### Reproduction

```ts
import { ApplicationRef, Component, computed, signal } from '@angular/core';
import { TestBed } from '@angular/core/testing';
import { Combobox, ComboboxPopup, ComboboxWidget } from '@angular/aria/combobox';
import { Listbox, Option } from '@angular/aria/listbox';

@Component({
  imports: [Combobox, ComboboxPopup, ComboboxWidget, Listbox, Option],
  template: `
    <input ngCombobox #combobox="ngCombobox" [alwaysExpanded]="true" [(value)]="query" />
    <ng-template ngComboboxPopup [combobox]="combobox">
      <div ngListbox ngComboboxWidget #listbox="ngListbox" focusMode="activedescendant"
        selectionMode="explicit" [activeDescendant]="listbox.activeDescendant()"
        (valueChange)="changes.push($event)">
        @for (o of shown(); track o) {
          <div ngOption [value]="o" [label]="o">{{ o }}</div>
        }
      </div>
    </ng-template>
  `,
})
class Repro {
  readonly query = signal('');
  readonly shown = computed(() => ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry'].filter((o) => o.includes(this.query())));
  readonly changes: (readonly string[])[] = [];
}

it('Enter in the render that filters selects the removed option', async () => {
  const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(Repro);
  document.body.appendChild(fixture.nativeElement);
  const stable = () => TestBed.inject(ApplicationRef).whenStable();
  await stable();
  const input = fixture.nativeElement.querySelector('input') as HTMLInputElement;
  input.focus();

  input.value = 'cherry';                                   // filters out apple, the active option
  input.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
  input.dispatchEvent(new KeyboardEvent('keydown', { key: 'Enter', bubbles: true })); // same task
  await stable();
  await stable();

  expect(fixture.componentInstance.changes).toEqual([['cherry']]);
  // Actual: [['apple'], []]; the DOM shows only 'cherry'.
  // With `await stable()` between the input event and Enter: [['cherry']].
});
```

We saw it in Chromium too (Playwright, the last letter and Enter sent in one burst through CDP): 7 runs in 10 selected the removed option.

### Expected behavior

Enter selects the option that is rendered and active, or does nothing when the active option has been removed. It never selects or emits an option that is no longer in the list.

### Actual behavior

`valueChange` emits the removed option (`['apple']`), then `[]` a render later.

### Environment

- Angular: 22.2.3 (`@angular/aria` 22.2.3; the relevant code is unchanged in 22.2.4)
- Zoneless
- Chromium (Playwright 1.64) and jsdom (Vitest 5)

### Workaround

Ignore a `valueChange` whose value is not among the options currently rendered.
